  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Quiet weather and warming trend prior to cold\nfront.\n\nFollowing the remains of Isaias, the weather is expected to be \nbenign until the end of the work week. Isaias is still bringing some \ngusty winds up to 40 knots but as it (or what's left of \nit) moves further away, the winds are expected to calm down by \nthis evening. \n\nA 500 mb ridge near the Texas coast will makes its way to the \nsoutheast later this week. This will introduce more \nwesterly/southwesterly flow and an increase in heights which \nwill bring more warm and moist air from the Gulf. Some cloud \ndebris from Hurricane Simon west of Mexico in the Pacific may \nalso be transported to the east coast. The higher pressure will \nstill result in mostly clear skies, but it will be unseasonably\nwarm and humid. High temperatures are forecast to reach into \nthe upper 80s in the Piedmont on Friday. Dew points are likely \nto go back into the 60s and reach 70. While the heat index is \ncurrently not expected to hit 90 (assuming the ridge center \ndoesn't move north) it will still be rather warm by October \nstandards.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain possible by Friday and colder weather this \nweekend.\n\nA large trough carrying a surface cold front is expected to \nswoop through the Mid-Atlantic between Friday night and Saturday\nmorning. This will also coincide with the next best chance of \nrainfall but the highest likelihood will likely be in the \nwestern mountainous counties. A few hundred CAPE and shear of \n20-30 knots may support some storm clusters with damaging wind \ngusts but it is too early to know what the exact hazards are.\n\nWhat is worth noting is the new air mass that will follow the front. \nConsiderably drier and colder air will rush in as wind gusts jump \nbetween 25-35 mph along and west of the Blue Ridge and up to 40\nmph at ridgetops. There could be as high as a 20 degree \ntemperature difference between Friday and Saturday as Saturday \nafternoon highs may only get into the lower 50s to lower 60s. \nSunday morning, so far, looks to be the coldest with lows in \nthe upper 20s to lower 30s. This could be the first freeze of \nthe season for a good chunk of the area so this will be \nmonitored for potential impacts.",
